node_modules/
.venv/
.env
.env.*
.env.DEV
.env.local
backend/.env
backend/.env.*
__pycache__/
**__pycache__**
**.pyc
*.pyo
.vscode/

# Allow .env.production / .env.demo (per-channel Vite build config). These hold
# only public VITE_* values -- the channel WS URL + Firebase WEB config, which is
# client-side and non-secret -- so committing them makes both channel builds
# reproducible on any checkout. Real secrets (e.g. GEMINI_API_KEY) never live
# here; they go in Secret Manager.
!.env.production
!.env.demo
# schema-editor/.env.hosted (hosted-mode build config, bd 92qe.2) is the same
# class: only public VITE_* values (delivery-mode flag + the backend URL). The
# access code is NEVER baked here -- contractors enter it in the UI (or pass
# VITE_EDITOR_TOKEN via the shell env at build time, off-repo). Scoped to the
# schema-editor PATH on purpose (Codex P1 r2, PR #280): a bare !.env.hosted
# would un-ignore a file of that name ANYWHERE -- including backend/, where
# .env* files are exactly where real secrets live.
!schema-editor/.env.hosted
# admin-console/.env.hosted (bd polyglot-pdf-b6943) is the SAME class as
# schema-editor's above, for the same reason: only public VITE_* values (the
# admin service's Cloud Run URL + the Firebase web config, all client-side
# and non-secret). Access is gated by the app_admin custom claim, never a
# token baked into this file. Scoped to the admin-console PATH for the same
# reason as schema-editor's entry.
!admin-console/.env.hosted

# example data files
example_webforms/

# Test artifacts
.pytest_cache/
# Coverage data + reports (pytest-cov / coverage.py + vitest coverage-v8)
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
**/coverage/

# Logs (generated files)
.logs/
*.log
backend.log
backend/logs/
# Confirmation-audit logs (one JSONL per form session; never commit real ones)
logs/confirmations/

# Build outputs
.frontend/dist/
frontend/dist/
frontend/public/form-kits/

# Temporary files
tmp_*
*.tmp
# The schema editor's same-folder handshake marker (bd polyglot-pdf-s2bf). The
# browser writes it into the picked form-kits/ folder, the backend reads it back
# from its own root to prove both are looking at one directory, and the browser
# deletes it. Ignored because a crash mid-check can leave one behind, and it is
# per-run scratch either way.
.editor-store-check.json
# The same marker for the EXTRACTOR channel (bd polyglot-pdf-ball), read by the
# Vite dev host instead of the backend. A SEPARATE file from the one above on
# purpose (bd polyglot-pdf-bagn): the two checks run unserialized against the
# same folder, so sharing one name let either channel's cleanup delete the
# other's in-flight marker and refuse a folder that genuinely agrees.
.editor-extract-check.json
# Ad-hoc rescue/backup copies made while hand-correcting a schema. The kit's
# real provenance is schema_archived.json + migration_report.json (the
# migrate_schema_v2.py backup convention) plus git history -- a *.bak snapshot
# is a working-copy artifact that must never reach the tree.
*.json.bak
# acroform_dump.py scratch output landing in a kit dir. The dump is regenerable
# from the kit's form.pdf in one command, so a temp copy is never worth
# committing. ANCHORED to form-kits/ on purpose: an unanchored *_acroform.json
# would also swallow a future backend/tests/fixtures/<kit>_acroform.json, which
# is a natural name for a committed test input.
form-kits/*/*_acroform.json

# Instruction booklets (IRS/USCIS PDFs). These are large, freely re-downloadable
# upstream documents that the enrichment tooling reads from the kit dir. Both
# globs below mirror scripts/authoring/enrich_instructions.py::find_booklet,
# which accepts *instructions*.pdf AND the abbreviated *instr*.pdf shape (its
# docstring cites i-90instr.pdf) -- matching only the long form would leave the
# abbreviated one tracked.
# Only w7-instructions.pdf predates this rule and stays tracked; new booklets are
# kept LOCAL so a multi-megabyte binary does not land in a repo with no LFS.
form-kits/*/*instructions*.pdf
form-kits/*/*instr*.pdf
!form-kits/w7/w7-instructions.pdf

# Superseded kit revisions (bd polyglot-pdf-w3zw). The archive lives in the GCS
# bucket -- the authenticated store scripts/read_kit_revision.py reads -- and is
# deliberately NOT a git artifact: scripts/pull-form-kits.sh rsyncs the bucket
# back into this tree, so without this rule every superseded schema (hundreds of
# KB each, one per republish) would be committed by the workflow that script's
# own header prescribes. ANCHORED to form-kits/ for the same reason as the
# acroform rule above: an unanchored _archive/ would swallow unrelated dirs.
form-kits/*/_archive/

# Draft-save snapshots (bd polyglot-pdf-y2l0p). Same reasoning as _archive
# above, and the same round trip: the local store's default root IS this
# git-tracked tree (backend/config.py FORM_KITS_LOCAL_ROOT), so in local mode
# every editor save writes a snapshot straight into the working copy -- up to
# 10 per kit, of drafts nobody intended to version. ANCHORED to form-kits/ for
# the same reason.
form-kits/*/_drafts/

# Beads / Dolt files (added by bd init)
.dolt/
*.db
.beads-credential-key
.beads/embeddeddolt/
.beads/.auto-import-*.jsonl
# dolt CLI cached by scripts/beads_remote_bootstrap.py (remote-session hydration)
.beads/.cache/
# transient R2 drain/handover runtime artifacts (drain markers + handover notes
# written by scripts/drain_flag.py); session-scoped, never source of truth
.beads/.runtime/
# retired A/B experiment counter cache (bd polyglot-pdf-40r4 removed the ledger
# that wrote it). Kept ignored so a leftover copy on any existing checkout stays
# invisible to closeout's dirty-tree gate instead of surfacing as untracked.
.beads/experiment_state.json
.venv-backend/
backend/.venv

# bd polyglot-pdf-jnjj.39: --debug measurement artifacts hold model transcripts +
# the exact field values written (user data / PHI). The cost harness is a
# never-deployed spike, but these must NEVER be committed regardless of the run's
# --out-dir (a repo-wide rule -- scripts/spikes/.gitignore only covered the default
# location; Codex PR #246). The committable cost CSV/meta.json siblings are NOT
# matched by this pattern.
*.debug.json

# Local project-scoped MCP server config (e.g. the gemini-docs MCP). Machine-
# local developer setup, not shared project state -- keep it out of the tree.
.mcp.json
scripts/authoring/outputs/

# Scratch pitch-deck work-in-progress (maintainer's local sandbox for pitch
# material that isn't ready to share/commit). Everything else under pitch/ is
# tracked as normal.
pitch/ignored_pitch/

# FORM KITS ARE NOT A GIT ARTIFACT (maintainer, 2026-09-03).
#
# The GCS bucket is the CANONICAL collection. Authors publish to it from the
# hosted schema editor, and the deployed backend serves kit bytes straight out
# of it (GET /api/form-kits/{id}/schema|pdf -- form_kit_routes.py). This local
# form-kits/ tree is a SCRATCH SPACE for rapid local work before publishing;
# it is not a second store, and nothing should be committed here.
#
# Committing a kit is actively harmful, not merely redundant:
#   - it forks the canonical copy, so the tree and the bucket silently diverge
#     (that divergence is what broke the deployed I-90 -- bd polyglot-pdf-6p77c);
#   - LocalFormKitStore synthesizes its manifest by SCANNING directories and
#     treats absent metadata as public, so a half-finished kit becomes a phantom
#     form in every developer's local catalogue;
#   - it binds tests to a specific form, which is the churn bd polyglot-pdf-1mgkq
#     exists to remove. At the hundreds-to-thousands of kits this is heading for,
#     a per-form artifact in git does not scale.
#
# Pull the bucket down with scripts/pull-form-kits.sh when you need kits locally.
#
# w7 and f1040 stay TRACKED for now and are the exception, not the pattern
# (w9-heldout is also tracked but is NOT a kit -- it is a deliberately held-out
# PDF with no schema.json, see its NOTE.md, and _translation is shared glossary
# data rather than a form):
# f1040 is NOT in the bucket (git is currently its only copy, so untracking it
# would delete it), and both still back kit-coupled tests. Migrating them --
# publish to the bucket, then untrack -- is bd polyglot-pdf-1mgkq's job, since
# it has to land with the test work that stops depending on them. Do NOT add a
# third.
#
# HAZARD THIS RULE DOES NOT COVER (bd polyglot-pdf-xyfu8): `git` ignoring a kit
# does NOT stop a deploy publishing it. deploy-all.sh's --form-kits stage
# enumerates form-kits/ from the FILESYSTEM and gates only on
# metadata.status == "complete" -- it has no git-awareness at all. So an
# UNTRACKED scratch kit left at status:complete uploads to the shared bucket and
# lands in the demo's form list, which is the label-collision failure the old
# narrow i-90_v2 rule warned about. The blanket rule widens the untracked set,
# so it widens that exposure: before running a --form-kits deploy, check what is
# actually about to publish (a kit dir with status:complete that `git ls-files`
# does not list is the shape to look for). Staging and demo share the bucket.
form-kits/*/
!form-kits/_translation/
!form-kits/w9-heldout/
!form-kits/w7/
!form-kits/f1040/
